model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A code update hands the pool controller over without cutting a model stream. Only the deployed entrypoint's signed adoption read can start it, and only for a controller code different from the object's own; repeated reads change nothing. The object then refuses new model calls, app model calls included, at the step where it counts them, with the retryable 503 and a handover marker. It waits within a bound taken from the pool's measured stream durations, kept well inside the platform's own reset, for its counted finite model calls to end and for any alarm pass, Home pass or runtime handoff to finish; model sockets are not counted, and the reset closes them for their clients to reconnect. It records the handover, and resets itself so its next event runs the deployed code. The handover never stops or starts the container; that the platform's reset keeps it and its boot is for the stage proof to show. A new instance that still runs the old code finds the recent record and does not drain again. The entrypoint sends a refused safe read or held model call again each second inside its replay window, so a caller whose wait ends inside it sees nothing; past it the caller gets the retryable 503. The drain lives in memory and ends by itself past the bound. One POOL_CODE_HANDOVER line per handover names its end reason, the requests in flight at its start and at its end, and how long it waited."
      constraint "The pool controller says what happened to it. Each construction writes one POOL_CONTROLLER_INSTANCE line with an isolate identifier named on the isolate's first construction, the time since that first construction, a construction count and whether the container was already running. A new isolate shows a platform replacement or a deploy; a later construction in the same isolate shows a rebuilt object. A model stream whose upstream read fails, in the controller or a fleet cell, writes one POOL_STREAM_BROKEN line per failure however many stacked wrappers see it, none for a close the pool makes on purpose, and at most three a second. The line carries the place it broke and the error's closed name, code and message class and the platform's retryable, overloaded, remote and reset flags, never its text. The native stream wrapper scans each read once and parses at most 4,194,304 characters of one unfinished event; past that it stops parsing and passes the rest through, re-encoded as UTF-8, so one endless event cannot hold the isolate's memory. Past that point a failure cuts the stream with no retry terminal, which the model clients retry by themselves."
    }
  }
}
